        ; body)` both evaluate their SECOND item exactly once, before the
        ; loop, because the parser hoists it into `for$v`/`for$lo`
        ; (`parseForExpr`). The third is `hi` in one shape and the BODY
        ; in the other, and this predicate sees the head and the index
        ; but not the arity, so it answers false there rather than
        ; guess; the binder at 1 and the body at 4 are not expressions
        ; evaluated once either.
